      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Right to Repair movement is fundamentally about consumer empowerment and fighting corporate monopolies. Proponents argue that restricting repairs forces consumers into expensive, planned obsolescence, which is an anti-consumer practice. Manufacturers, conversely, argue that proprietary designs protect their intellectual property and ensure product safety, which is a legitimate business concern. Ultimately, the debate hinges on whether corporate control over repair outweighs the consumer's right to own and maintain their property.",
      "source": "target",
